Title: Jubilee
Order: 8
Production Code: 1x08

INT. BROWN'S CAMP, THOMSON'S HUT - DAY

PAGE: 1

PANEL: A low hut, light through the branches. Thomson on a cot, paralyzed in every limb, head propped, face grey; his mind sane and his voice strong. Warren stands at the cot with a glass of liquor.

THOMSON
No, 'tain't no use holdin' out.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
Bring in the gal and Judah, an' take down every word I say if you want the gal to have her own.

PANEL: Close on Thomson, flat and calculating.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
You're a lawyer, ain't you? Sent out here on the Carlingford case, warn't you?

THOMSON (CONT'D)
Never struck you that me and the Colonel knew where to find the man you was huntin', did it?

PANEL: Warren, mind in a tumult, holds a glass to the dying man's lips; with his free hand he beckons the doorway.

PANEL: Winona, Judah and Steward come in; Brown and his sons standing as witnesses in the background. Winona sits with clasped hands folded on her breast.

PANEL: Thomson, with the transient vigor of the liquor, opens his eyes.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
I'm here yet, Judah; I almost got the one chance you offered me, but it ain't for long I'll hender you.

PAGE: 2

PANEL: Steward kneels and begins a fervent prayer; nobody else speaks; Thomson's eyes follow the voice.

PANEL: Close on Thomson's face; a faint comprehension spreading in the sin-hardened eyes.

THOMSON
That's all right, Parson. I know I deviled you an' tried to kill you; I did the same to the nigger, an' to Maxwell.

PANEL: Thomson, looking toward Winona, with something near shame.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
But I done the girl worse 'n dirt. I'm goin' to try to make the damage I've done good, to the girl, anyhow.

PANEL: Steward, muttering, nodding, not noting the profanity.

STEWARD
Miserable sinners, miserable sinners, all of us. God forgive us.

PANEL: Warren sits with paper and ink-pot on his knee, hands shaking, scarcely able to hold the pen.

THOMSON (O.P.)
Take down every word I say, Mr. Maxwell, an' let me kiss the Book that it's all true.

PANEL: Thomson's lips on the cover of a Bible Steward holds to him.

PAGE: 3 (TURN)

PANEL: Warren, pen ready; Winona beside him watching with huge eyes.

WARREN
Go on.

PANEL: Thomson, now speaking in well-bred phrases, his Southwestern drawl dropped like a coat.

THOMSON
Mr. Maxwell, you came to America to find the lost Captain Henry Carlingford, heir to the great Carlingford estates.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
You thought you were on a hopeless quest, did you not?

PANEL: Warren nods once.

PANEL: Close on Thomson, the clearest word of the day.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
You found the captain all right, but you never knew it. White Eagle was the man you wanted!

PANEL: The whole room: Winona in tears, Brown's sons looking at each other, Steward's hand to his mouth, Judah rising with the old ferocious glitter in his eyes.

JUDAH
And so you murdered him! I have suspected as much for two years.

PAGE: 4

PANEL: Thomson, with a weak shake of the head.

THOMSON
No, no, Judah; I wasn't in that. Titus did the killing.

PANEL: Flashback, dim green-grey like the earlier flashbacks: the island at dusk, a bend in the shore, a tall man crouched in the brush with a rifle, his face turned from us; in the clearing, White Eagle with his back to him bending over a canoe.

SFX: KRAK

PANEL: Warren writing rapidly, questioning the man closely.

WARREN
Why did Colonel Titus commit this murder? How came you to know this?

PANEL: Thomson, with great effort.

THOMSON
Titus hated him because he stood between him and a vast fortune. And he was jealous of his wife's love for Henry Carlingford.

PANEL: Flashback: a slender young Captain Henry in uniform and a girl in white (Miss Venton) walking arm in arm along a garden path.

THOMSON (V.O.)
He was her lover from childhood, and she loved him until death.

PAGE: 5

PANEL: Warren, stern and solemn, pen still moving.

WARREN
Then tell me who killed young Lord George. Miss Venton was affianced to him.

WARREN (CONT'D)
You can tell if you will, for Miss Venton married Colonel Titus.

PANEL: Thomson mutters to himself; all breathless; the dying face working.

PANEL: Thomson, a mutter.

THOMSON
No, it won't neither.

PANEL: Thomson looks up at the circle of faces.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
Everything must be made clear?

WARREN
Yes, if you wish to help this poor girl whom you have wronged so cruelly.

PANEL: Steward lays a hand on the dying man's shoulder.

STEWARD
It won't be against you when you get on the other side, Thomson. Free your mind, my friend; it'll do you good.

PANEL: Thomson, a weak, wry look.

THOMSON
I'll take your word for it, Parson, but I never was much on religion. Well, then. I killed Lord George.

PAGE: 6

PANEL: Flashback: a great English house, evening. A young THOMSON, clean-shaven in a valet's black coat, laying out clothes for a handsome fair young man (Young Lord George) before a mirror.

THOMSON (V.O.)
I swore to bring disgrace upon the entire Carlingford family. And I have done it; I have had a rich revenge.

PANEL: Flashback: the same house; a pretty maid in an apron with a dark ribbon (Thomson's sister) at a window, her hand on her mouth, as Young Lord George kisses her hand.

THOMSON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I was Lord George's valet; my sister, Miss Venton's maid. Lord George could never resist a pretty face.

PANEL: Flashback: a London street at night, rain, a woman in a torn shawl in a doorway; and a young man in black coming up the pavement and stopping.

THOMSON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
He deceived her, and when he tired of his toy abandoned her to the usual fate of such women.

THOMSON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I found her when it was too late.

PANEL: Flashback: the young valet with his sister's shawl in his fists, face working, a vow.

THOMSON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
And I swore revenge so long as one lived with a drop of the blood in his veins.

PAGE: 7

PANEL: Flashback: a drawing-room, two brothers face to face, hands raised in argument; a valet at a half-open door.

THOMSON (V.O.)
One day the brothers quarrelled bitterly over Miss Venton. Then was my chance.

PANEL: Flashback: a dark corridor, Young Lord George walking away; behind him the valet with a pistol raised.

SFX: BANG

PANEL: Flashback: Young Lord George falling forward on a carpet; a blurred servant's figure running.

THOMSON (V.O.)
I shot Lord George in the back, and fled, knowing that suspicion would fall on Capt. Henry.

PANEL: Back in the hut. Thomson's voice thinning.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
It did; and two of my enemies were out of my way. The Captain lived an outcast among savages for years.

PANEL: Thomson, with the last strength he has.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
For the sake of his daughter Lillian, Colonel Titus killed White Eagle and held Winona as a slave,

THOMSON (CONT'D)
thus cutting off the last direct heir to Carlingford.

PAGE: 8

PANEL: Thomson fading, to Warren.

THOMSON
Will you call it square, young fellow? You and Winona, and Judah? I've done you bad, but I've told the truth at last.

PANEL: Thomson, a ghost of a grin.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
Mr. Maxwell, I reckon you'll marry the heiress. I'm glad.

PANEL: Thomson, whispering.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
Land in Canidy soon, boys; they'll be after you inside a week. Big Government force.

PANEL: Warren, voice trembling in spite of himself; rising and joining Winona and Judah at the bedside.

WARREN
Why, surely. I'm awfully grateful to you for telling me this; it makes my work easy.

PANEL: Thomson relapsing into his old vernacular.

THOMSON
I sort o' hated to tell, fer a fac', but I'm glad I done it.

PANEL: A grey shadow creeps over the white face; the cot; stillness. Steward lays a blanket over it.

PAGE: 9

PANEL: The hut: all kneeling by the bed with bowed heads; Steward's voice in prayer.

STEWARD
Let us pray.

PANEL: Outside the hut door the crack of rifles; birds rising from the trees.

SFX: KRAKRAKRAKRAK

CAPTION: It was the volley that carried death to the last of the captured Rangers.

PANEL: Maybee in his bed in the next hut, hearing the story from Steward; no surprise on his face.

MAYBEE
These happenings 'min' me o' the words o' the Psalmist: "Thy right hand shall teach thee terrible things."

PANEL: Steward, with a sigh.

STEWARD
Amen. But full of mercy, also, since they will deliver this poor girl from the hand of the spoiler.

PANEL: Winona and Judah at Maybee's bedside, one at each hand, applying poultices; bringing their Indian knowledge of medicine to bear.

PAGE: 10 (TURN)

PANEL: Maybee, propped up, a faraway look at the roof.

MAYBEE
My children, I believe I can match that story o' Thomson's. I have a surprisin' secret to unfol' to you.

PANEL: Flashback: White Eagle on the porch of the island cottage at sunset, a long tin box on his knees, Maybee on the step beside him.

WHITE EAGLE
Maybee, keep this here package if anything comes across me, 'till my girl's a responsible age.

PANEL: Maybee, shaking his head.

MAYBEE (V.O.)
After he was dead I said to myself, in the words of Scripter,

MAYBEE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
"a charge to keep I have." An' 'tain't safe to keep it.

PANEL: Flashback: Maybee at the hotel office, handing the tin box across the counter to a startled Ma' Jane.

MAYBEE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
So I give the package to Ma' Jane an' she has it unto this day.

PANEL: Close on Winona's face, uncomprehending; she looks at Warren and at Judah.

EXT. THE ROAD EAST - DAY

PAGE: 11

PANEL: A week later: a wagon train on a dusty road; Maybee on a bed of straw in the wagon, improvised ambulance, bandaged, sitting up. Warren riding at the head; Winona and Judah behind.

CAPTION: A week later our fugitives started for Canada via Buffalo, N. Y., by a circuitous path well known to Captain Brown.

PANEL: The abandoned Brown camp: huts burned, the creek running over black ashes; Government troops with muskets walking through it.

CAPTION: The Brown camp was deserted, and the Government troops, when they arrived, found only the blackened remains of the once busy settlement.

PANEL: A hill; the grass growing over the sod where the Rangers had been buried, as if it had never been disfigured.

CAPTION: The last gun had been fired in Kansas by Brown's forces, and he was next heard of in the Virginia insurrection which ended so fatally for the intrepid leader.

PANEL: The wagons crossing a river; the Canadian shore green in the distance.

CAPTION: The other refugees had passed in safety over the border into Canada, and the fugitive slaves were, at last, rejoicing on free soil.

EXT. GRAND ISLAND HOTEL - MORNING

PAGE: 12

PANEL: Morning. The hotel's piazza empty, the usual hangers-on not about. A small party of bronzed and bearded riders reins up at the steps, with the Brown family, and a wagon. Maybee sits up in it and bellows.

MAYBEE
Ma' Jane! Ma'--Jane!

PANEL: A woman's voice from the rear of the house; then MA' JANE herself bursts round the corner with her arms akimbo, big and plump, followed by Aunt Vinnie the cook and 'Tavius.

MA' JANE
Ya'as! What's up neow?

PANEL: Ma' Jane stops dead, staring at the company.

MA' JANE (CONT'D)
Law sakes! If it ain't Ebenezer--an' the Englishman--an' Jude!--an' 'Nona!!

PANEL: Ma' Jane has folded Winona in her arms; the two rock together in tears.

MA' JANE (CONT'D)
My precious child! Thank heaven we've got you back safe! It's been an awful time fer you.

PANEL: Maybee in the wagon, wiping his eyes, indignant, thumping his bandage.

MAYBEE
Here's me, wyounded an' dyin', an' when I git home the wife of my bosom ain't no eyes fer me nor tears nuther.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
I enter a suit for devorce at once.

PAGE: 13

PANEL: Ma' Jane, one hand on a hip, looking at him with great tenderness and no sympathy at all.

MA' JANE
You ain't no call to be jealous, Maybee, as you well know. Ef you're sick, I'll nuss you; ef you're hungry, I'll feed you.

PANEL: Ma' Jane turning to the household, bossing.

MA' JANE (CONT'D)
Come in, everybody. 'Tavius, git a move on with them hosses! Vinnie, stop your grinnin' an' hustle with the dinner.

PANEL: 'Tavius marching off with the horses, grinning; Vinnie making for the kitchen; neighbors already coming across the grass.

PANEL: The hotel office and piazza packed with leading citizens, hats in their hands, listening; Warren telling the story at the center of it.

CAPTION: The news of Maybee's arrival with Winona and Judah had been industriously circulated by 'Tavius.

PANEL: Maybee, with a cup of coffee, pointing at the stairs.

MAYBEE
Now, Ma' Jane, you remember the papers I gave you?

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
I want you to fetch 'em out and give 'em to the child before us all.

PAGE: 14

PANEL: Ma' Jane bringing a long tin box out of a cupboard behind the bar and placing it in her husband's hand.

PANEL: Maybee opens the lid and shows the contents: three legal papers folded and sealed, a few pieces of jewelry.

MAYBEE
Let's see. Three legal dockymen's and a few pieces of jewelry. Them's 'em, I reckon. There you, my girl.

PANEL: Winona takes the papers without speaking; a dizziness; she hands them straight to Warren.

WINONA
Read them. I cannot.

PANEL: Close on Warren's hands unfolding the paper that lies on top, like a thick letter: a certificate of marriage and birth under a broken seal.

PANEL: The jewelry spread on a table: jeweled family portraits including a young Captain Henry in uniform; a ring with a crest.

CAPTION: Nothing was missing; the chain of evidence was complete, even to the trained eye of the legal critic.

PANEL: Warren, rising with the certificate in his hand, to the packed room.

PAGE: 15

PANEL: Congratulations: hands everywhere, shaking Warren's, Winona's, Judah's; neighbors, black and white.

PANEL: A heavy-set man in a black coat steps up from the crowd, hat in hand: the REPRESENTATIVE TO CONGRESS, JAMESON.

JAMESON
I for one, from this day out, condemn this cursed "system" of ours.

JAMESON (CONT'D)
We're a laughing stock for the whole world, to say nothing of the wickedness of the thing.

PANEL: A voice from the crowd, cheerful.

CROWD VOICE
Right you are, Jameson. Put them sentiments down for every man of us.

PANEL: Judah, who can say nothing, wrings Warren's hand hard; Warren returning it.

WARREN
You go with us to England, Judah, and share prosperity as you have shared adversity.

PANEL: Warren, firm, with a hand on Judah's shoulder.

WARREN (CONT'D)
You shall choose your own path in life and be a man among men.

PAGE: 16

PANEL: Maybee, huskily, to Winona, a big hand on her head.

MAYBEE
I ain't any words to say, my girl; but you know what's in my ol' heart.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
I know you won't forget us when you're a great lady.

PANEL: Maybee, to the room.

MAYBEE (CONT'D)
Poor White Eagle, he had a rocky time of it, sure.

PANEL: A canoe sliding over the blue lake in a gold light, three figures: Warren and Judah at paddles, Winona leaning over the side trailing a hand; Niagara booming beyond the water.

CAPTION: Many visits were made to the island by our three friends before the day when they embarked from Canada for old England.

PANEL: On the ridge, the sun-flecked woods; Winona kneels in the grass and parts the leaves. The Indian-pipes with their faint pink stems.

CAPTION: The Indian-pipes with their faint pink stems lay concealed among the bushes as of old.

PAGE: 17

PANEL: Beneath the great pine that shaded White Eagle's grave: the three of them resting, reverent. Winona with her face buried in her lover's bosom.

PANEL: Warren holding her close, one hand on her hair.

WARREN
My heart's dearest, you must not grieve; your time of mourning is past. He is happy now as he sees your future assured.

PANEL: Warren, quietly.

WARREN (CONT'D)
Through you he has conquered death and the grave; justice and honor are his after many years of shame.

PANEL: Winona lifts her face, comforted.

CAPTION: They loved each other; they would marry sooner or later, after they reached England, with the sanction of her grandfather, old Lord George.

PANEL: A ship at the dock at Montreal, flags in a light breeze; the three at the rail looking back.

CAPTION: American caste prejudice could not touch them in their home beyond the sea.

PAGE: 18

PANEL: A hall in England: Judah in a plain dark coat, a Queen's officer in red before him with a sword, a ribbon, a line of dignitaries.

CAPTION: Judah never returned to America.

CAPTION: After the news of John Brown's death had aroused the sympathies of all christendom for the slaves, he entered the service of the Queen.

PANEL: Judah, older, in a court dress, rising from his knee, a sword on his shoulder.

CAPTION: His daring bravery and matchless courage brought its own reward; he was knighted; had honors and wealth heaped upon him.

PANEL: A formal wedding party on a lawn; Judah with a woman in white; a great house behind them.

CAPTION: He finally married into one of the best families of the realm.

PANEL: Winona in an English drawing-room reading a letter at a desk, a lawn outside; in her face, fortified and serene.

CAPTION: Winona celebrated in her letters to Mr. Maybee the wonders of her life in England, where all worshipped the last beautiful representative of an ancient family.

PAGE: 19 (SPLASH)

PANEL: Full-page splash: the yard behind the Grand Island Hotel at evening, people sitting on the grass and the steps, white and black neighbors, a red sun going down behind the lake; Aunt Vinnie on her back step with her arms raised, mid-sermon, her whole face lit. In the foreground bottom edge, a cluster of pale pink Indian-pipes pushing through the grass.
LIGHT: Red sun low across the lake

AUNT VINNIE
Glory to God, we's boun' to be free.

PAGE: 20

PANEL: The same yard, nearer: Aunt Vinnie turns from the neighbors, who have gone quiet, and points back up the lane toward the hotel's window, where a lamp has been lit.

AUNT VINNIE
Dar's dat gal, she's got black blood nuff in her to put her on de block in this fersaken country.

AUNT VINNIE (CONT'D)
But over dar she's a lady with de top crus' of de crus'. Somethin's gwine happen.

PANEL: Aunt Vinnie, one hand on the kitchen door-post, looking up at the evening sky with the first star out.

AUNT VINNIE
Jestice been settin' on de sprangles ob de sun a long time watchin' dese people how dey cuts der shines.

PANEL: She goes into the kitchen and the door half closes; the neighbors on the grass listening to her voice carry back across the yard.

CAPTION: Ole Satan's mad, an' I am glad, send de angels down.

CAPTION: Dis is de year of Jubilee. De Lord has come to set us free, O, send dem angels down.

PANEL: Last panel, wide and quiet: the island at dusk across the water, the great pine black against the sky; and in the foreground, in the grass, the faint pink stems of the Indian-pipes, untouched.

CAPTION: The End.
